# MONKEY BAITING

A 2D fighting game (Georgian-era monkey-baiting booth) being ported from a
self-contained JS implementation at `web/monkey-baiting/` onto this repo's
C17/Flecs engine, together with a batch **tournament simulator** that scores
balance candidates.

Architecture of record:
`web/monkey-baiting/docs/specs/tournament-simulator-architecture.md`.
Reference implementation: `web/monkey-baiting/logic.js` plus
`web/monkey-baiting/data/`. It is a **read-only reference for behaviour and
data, never an oracle** - where its float arithmetic and this port's
fixed-point contract disagree, fixed point wins (architecture ruling 7). JS
parity is an explicit non-goal.

## Port status

**Stage 1 (done): skeleton to stepping.** Component schema, integer-state
contract, rules loader, batch world, eleven-system frame order, match/round
lifecycle.

**Stage 2 (done): the fight.** Real gameplay on real data - locomotion
(walk, jump, wall scramble, retreat shuffle), the attack state machine
(startup/active/recovery, multi-hit, leaps, move_forward), hit resolution
(block/chip, stance counter, armour, clean hit with Q16.16 knockback and
precomputed launch sin/cos), grabs and the chain yank, hazards (rats, coin
zones, thrown objects), THE CROSS and the odds flip, the finisher window and
cinematic, separation, the KO fall, and the AI: the four ported strategies
(`default`, `spam`, `random`, `passive`) driven by the bot weight/proximity
tables. Every gameplay constant is authored data; nothing is defaulted in C.

**Stage 2b (done): perfect-policy playback.** The last stubbed seam is
closed - the simulator loads the solver's equilibrium policies and plays
them, so it can now score against them. See "Perfect-policy playback" below.

**Stage 4 (done): multi-world threading.** `--threads N` runs N private
worlds on N OS threads over a deterministic partition of the canonical
queue. See "Threading" below.

**Stage S1 (done): the Python driver.** `tools/mb_driver.py` invokes `mb_sim`
with a pinned explicit configuration, parses `--json`, and writes a run record.
See "The Python driver" below.

**Stage S5 (done): the Python balance loop.** `tools/mb_loop/` scores a state
with the balance grammar, emits candidate patches, evaluates them against the
C simulator, and archives the lineage. See "The balance loop" below. This is
the stage the balance-run freeze was waiting on (architecture ruling 3).

**Stage S6 (done): full engine sandbox integration.** monkey-baiting is a
first-class engine game: registered in `engine/src/native_registry.c`,
`sandbox/select` -> `app/start` -> `sim/turn` builds and plays a match, the
whole match is visible and injectable over REST. See "Engine hosting" below.

**Stage S7 (done, pending a WASM build): the browser client.** The shipped
game rebuilt on the C core, with JavaScript surviving only as a presentation
shell. See "The WASM client" below. `emcc` is not on this box, so the wasm
compile, the served playtest and the retirement of `web/monkey-baiting/` are
outstanding.

**Stage S8 (done): the CUDA compile target.** `mb_sim --backend cuda` runs the
SAME step functions as a kernel launch over W lanes, with W as the launch
width. CPU and CUDA produce byte-identical result rows and result hash on the
full 36-entrant / 2520-match field. See "CUDA" below.

## Data is authored HERE

`sandboxes/monkey-baiting/data/` is THE authoring location for every
monkey-baiting number (2026-07-30, architecture S5 data single-sourcing).
The retired JS game and its frozen `web/monkey-baiting/data/` snapshot were
DELETED 2026-07-30 (spec 8.3 retirement checklist; recoverable from git
history; the campaign narrative YAML survives at
`web/monkey-baiting/docs/campaign-data/`). The balance loop patches only the
canonical files, and the model exporter (`tools/mb_loop/model_export.py`)
reads the character files from here -- or from a staged candidate through
`MB_DATA_DIR` -- and its ring geometry, chip ratio and roster from
`app.yaml`. Its byte-identity against the retired node exporter was the
acceptance gate that let the whole JS tree go.

## Layout

```
app.yaml                  sandbox config AND the simulator rules (ring, rounds,
                          combat constants, finisher, AI shape, roster order,
                          batch default, the `engine:` hosting block)
components/               component schema (YAML) + README.md, the
                          fixed-point contract - read it before touching state
entities/                 MbFighterUnit, MbMatchSlot, MbMatchRequest prefabs
data/characters/          character YAML (stats, mechanics, ai, move frame data)
data/strategies.yaml      the decision primitives and their params
data/balance.yaml         class envelopes: how far a tuning pass may move a
                          move's parameters (loop input, not runtime)
data/balance-grammar.yaml the balance grammar v3: situations, strategies,
                          patch templates, scoring weights (loop input)
data/balance-pins.yaml    47 pins: parameters the explorer may not touch
data/roster.yaml          the scored field, and each bot's grammar label
data/bots.yaml            bot weight/proximity tables over the strategies
systems/native/           the shared native system code (see below)
sim/main.c                mb_sim, the tournament simulator CLI
sim/mb_pool.c/.h          the multi-world thread pool (host-side, see below)
sim/mb_cuda.cu/.h         the CUDA backend (S8): mb_step.c under nvcc behind a
                          kernel launch; .h is the always-compilable interface
sim/mb_cuda_none.c        the same three symbols on a box with no CUDA toolkit,
                          every one of them a refusal (never a CPU fallback)
tools/mb_driver.py        the Python driver (S1) - invoke, parse, run record
tools/mb_loop/            the Python balance loop (S5) - score, emit, search
clients/wasm/             the browser client (S7): wasm_main.c + shell.js + chars.js
runs/                     loop archives (gitignored; regenerable)
tests/test_mb_sim.c       ctest suite (test_mb_sim)
tests/test_mb_cuda.c      ctest suite for the CPU-vs-CUDA gate (test_mb_cuda)
tests/test_mb_engine.c    ctest suite for engine hosting (test_mb_engine)
tests/test_mb_wasm.c      ctest suite for the WASM exports (test_mb_wasm)
tests/test_mb_driver.py   ctest suite for the driver (test_mb_driver)
tests/test_mb_loop.py     ctest suite for the loop (test_mb_loop)
```

## systems/native - one implementation, three consumers

| file | what | rules |
|---|---|---|
| `mb_fixed.h` | Q16.16 helpers | header-only, no libc |
| `mb_rng.h` | mulberry32 + FNV-1a, exact ports | header-only, no libc |
| `mb_types.h` | component structs, `MbRules`, `MbChar`, `MbMove`, `MbBotTable`, `MbBatch` | no flecs |
| `mb_step.c/.h` | **the simulation** - pure step functions | no flecs, no globals, no file-statics, no allocation, no libc |
| `mb_schema.c` | the C schema table (drift guard) | host |
| `mb_world.c/.h` | flecs registration, batch bind, harvest/refill | host |
| `mb_rules.c/.h` | YAML loading, entrant resolution | host |
| `mb_perfect.c/.h` | solved-policy loading (JSON -> `MbPolicy`) | host |
| `mb_engine.c/.h` | **engine hosting** - the eleven native systems, app.yaml `engine:` block, MbSpawnRequest drain | host; the ONLY file here that includes the engine |

**`mb_step.c` is compiler-portable by construction, and since S8 that is a
measured fact rather than an intention.** `sim/mb_cuda.cu` includes
`mb_step.c` VERBATIM under nvcc; the only things that differ are three macros
it defines first - `MB_INLINE` (the `mb_fixed.h`/`mb_rng.h` helpers), `MB_FN`
(the `mb_step.h` exports) and `MB_LOCAL` (the file-local helpers inside
`mb_step.c`). All three are `static` plus a device annotation on CUDA and
exactly `static` / `static inline` / nothing everywhere else, so none of them
can move a result row. The per-slot `mb_k_*` functions are the kernel bodies
and the `mb_step_*` sweeps are the launches, as designed. `MbBatch.count` is
the batch width W **and** the kernel launch width.

Four rules that are not negotiable in this directory:

1. **No file-static component-id caching.** Ids live in the per-world context
   (`MbWorld.ids`), always. `sandboxes/avk/systems/native/` caches them in
   file statics; that is process-global and would be raced by a
   world-per-core simulator.
2. **No floats in gameplay state.** See `components/README.md`.
3. **No silent fallbacks.** A missing rules key fails the load with the key
   name (root `CLAUDE.md` decision 12). Nothing is defaulted.
4. **No authored number in C.** Every gameplay constant arrives through
   `MbRules` (app.yaml) or `MbChar`/`MbMove` (data/characters). If a value
   is needed, it is added to the YAML and to the loader, never typed into a
   step function.

## Data and scaling

Fractional values are authored as decimal strings in YAML and converted
**once, at load**, to Q16.16 by exact integer arithmetic (`mb_parse_fp` -
no `strtod`, no float anywhere in the pipeline). Whole-frame counters, pixel
bounds, indices and counts stay plain integers. Probabilities become u32
thresholds pre-scaled to 2^32 and are compared against a raw RNG word.
Health fractions become absolute Q16.16 hp against the character's
`max_health`. Launch angles become Q16.16 sin/cos from a table of whole
degrees, so there is no runtime trigonometry on any target.

The constants that used to be literals inside `logic.js` are now authored in
`app.yaml` under `combat:` and `ai:` - the KO sprawl (`-3.2` / `2.2`), the two
velocity decays (`0.85` / `0.86`), the knockback shaping (`1.3`, `1.4`, `0.3`,
`0.4`), the freeze floors, the stance counter, separation, grab and yank
offsets, the walk-speed multipliers, the wall scramble, STAKES DOWN's step,
hazard geometry, the cross break, and the `default` strategy's shape
constants (`0.45`, `0.35`, `0.6`, `0.5`, and its range margins). Each is
commented at its definition with what it was in the reference.

**Data-driven caps (root `CLAUDE.md` decision 17).** `MB_MAX_MOVES`,
`MB_HAZARD_CAP` and `MB_MAX_CHARS` remain compile-time array bounds - the hot
path allocates nothing - but the loader asserts the real data against them
and fails loud: a character authoring more moves than `MB_MAX_MOVES`, a
`hazard_coins` move whose `coins_zones` exceeds `MB_HAZARD_CAP`, or a roster
longer than `MB_MAX_CHARS`. `test_mb_sim` re-checks all of it against the
loaded tables.

## The draw contract

Same seed -> same match, byte for byte, at any batch width. That guarantee is
the ORDER AND COUNT OF RNG DRAWS, not just the generator, and it is defined
here rather than inherited from `logic.js` (orchestrator ruling: no
draw-for-draw reproduction of the JS reference; JS agreement is not a
requirement).

Per match, per frame, draws are consumed in exactly this order:

1. **`ai_decide`, side 0 first, then side 1.** For each fighter:
   1. **the bot roll** - one draw, and only when the bot table can actually
      branch. It is taken when the component hold (`bots.hold_frames`)
      elapses, or the fighter's busy state flips, or nothing has been rolled
      yet. A *pure* bot (a single ungated entry) never rolls, and a table
      whose eligible weight sums to zero falls back to its heaviest ungated
      entry without drawing.
      The roll is `mb_rng_below(sum_of_eligible_weights)` walked against the
      prefix sums in table order - an integer draw, not a float times a
      float. Bot weights are therefore required to be positive integers.
   2. **the strategy decision**, but only on that strategy's decision frame.
      Cadences: `default` = `ai.decision_interval_frames` (16), `spam` = 1,
      `passive` = 1, `random` = 1 (it holds its own pick for
      `decision_interval` frames). Between decisions the previous input is
      replayed with the press bits dropped and the direction/block bits kept.
      Per-strategy draw order:
      - `default`: yank dodge, block reaction, stance, STAKES DOWN, special,
        heavy, light, spacing retreat, jump - each drawn only if its guard
        passes, and the function returns at the first one that fires.
      - `spam`: no draws at all.
      - `passive`: one block-reaction draw when a threat is winding up.
      - `random`: one attack-vs-movement draw, then exactly one index draw
        (over the ready moves, or over the five movement options).
      - `perfect`: **exactly one draw per DECISION, and only when the foe's
        quantized side state is FREE.** A committed or stunned foe is
        answered from `vsCommitted`, a pure best response that draws
        nothing. The cadence is 1 (it decides every actionable frame) but it
        paces itself: a frame on which the fighter is not in
        `idle`/`walk`/`block`/`jump` clears the plan and decides nothing,
        and a frame inside a held plan replays the action and decides
        nothing. Neither draws. So the ceiling is one draw per frame per
        fighter, and the steady state is one per `hold + 1` frames
        (`strategies.yaml` `perfect.params`: 6 frames for a walk, 9 for a
        block, 3 for a wait, 1 for a move press).
2. **`step_attack`** - hazard spawns draw here: one draw per coin zone in
   zone order, one draw per thrown object at each `projectile_interval`.
3. No other system draws.

Changing any of this invalidates every result corpus produced under
`sim_version`. It is a versioned contract, not an implementation detail.

## Entrant identity (final)

```
entrant id = "<char_id>:<bot_id>"        e.g. "jacco:house", "billy:chaos"
per-match seed = mulberry32( fnv1a("<entrant0_id>|<entrant1_id>|<seed_index>") )
```

`char_id` is a name from `app.yaml`'s `characters:` roster; `bot_id` is a key
of `bots.table` in `data/bots.yaml`. Sides swap on even seed indices, so the
id string of a pairing differs between its two orientations and each
orientation gets its own seed. `mb_rules_resolve_entrant` is the only way to
turn the string into indices, and it refuses loudly: a malformed id, an
unknown character, an unknown bot, a bot that can roll `perfect` while no
solved policies are loaded, or a `spam-<move>` component naming a move the
character does not author. Whether the specific ordered pairs a perfect
entrant needs are present is checked by `mb_world_set_queue`, which is the
only place that knows who is drawn against whom.

Every future corpus is keyed on these two rules. Append to the roster and to
the bot table; never reorder or rename.

## Perfect-policy playback

`perfect` plays a solved equilibrium policy, one per **ordered character
pair**, loaded from a directory given on the command line:

```bash
./build/local/Debug/mb_sim.exe --field "jacco:perfect,brock:house" --seeds 4 \
    --perfect web/monkey-baiting/policies-full-v3
```

Policy files (`perfect-<self>-vs-<foe>.json`: a `meta` block plus a
`bothFree` probability array and a `vsCommitted` action-index array) are
large and regenerable, so they are gitignored and live outside the sandbox
data dirs - hence the flag, never a compiled-in path. `mb_sim` loads exactly
the ordered pairs its field needs.

**Bots.** `bots.yaml` has three bots that can roll it: `perfect` (pure, the
scoring yardstick), `champion` and `gentle` (mixed). The entrant id needs no
new grammar - `"jacco:perfect"` is an ordinary `<char>:<bot>` id.

**Refusal, never demotion, in two places.** `mb_rules_resolve_entrant` takes
a `have_policies` flag and refuses a perfect-capable bot when no policy set
was loaded at all. Which *files* an entrant needs depends on who it is drawn
against, so the per-pair check lives in `mb_world_set_queue`, which sees the
pairings and refuses a queue whose entrant would need an absent policy. That
is what makes the runtime lookup total. A stale `meta.encoding` (anything but
3), a truncated array, an action naming an unknown move key, or a missing
`walk_to` action all fail the load loudly.

**The bucketing contract.** `gap` and the per-side retreat room INDEX the
policy table, so a one-unit difference selects a different action. Both are
therefore computed in **doubled Q16.16 units**, which is what makes them
exact: the two halvings the JS reference does in floating point (half the
summed hurtbox widths, half a hurtbox width) would each truncate 1/65536 px
in fixed point and could tip a bucket boundary. In doubled units they never
happen.

```
gap2  = 2*|x_foe - x_self| - (w_self + w_foe)
gq    = clamp( floor((gap2 + gapStep*2^16) / (gapStep*2^17)), 0, gapBuckets-1 )
back2 = 2*x_self - w_self - 2*ringLeft     (foe to the right)
      | 2*ringRight - 2*x_self - w_self    (foe to the left)
bb    = min( floor(max(0, back2) / (backStep*2^17)), backBuckets-1 )
row   = ((gq * backBuckets^2 + rq) * c0 + cd0) * c1 + cd1,  rq = bb_self*BB + bb_foe
```

The gap form is round-half-up written as a floor (`floor(g/s + 1/2) ==
floor((2g+s)/(2s))`); the back form is a floor of a non-negative quotient, so
C truncation *is* the floor. No division is inexact, so the bucket is a
function of the integer state alone. **This C definition is the contract**
(`sim_version` is the oracle; JS agreement is not required).

**The CDF.** `bothFree`'s probabilities are parsed by exact integer
arithmetic to 9 decimals (no `strtod`, no float in the pipeline) and each row
is normalised by its own total into a u32 cumulative distribution over 2^32,
consumed against one raw mulberry32 word. A cumulative bound of 2^32 does not
fit a u32 and is reached at the last action carrying probability, so it
**saturates** to `UINT32_MAX` rather than wrapping to 0 - truncating there
would make that action unreachable. The single top word falls through to the
last action: that is the rounding residue the final bucket absorbs, and it is
the deliberate, versioned divergence from the reference's float accumulation
(architecture spec 4.4).

**Memory.** One allocation per solved pair holds that pair's CDF and best
response tables back to back. The `MbPerfectSet` is loaded host-side once and
**shared read-only** by every match in every batch (and every world) through
`MbBatch.perfect` - nothing is copied per slot, and the step functions still
allocate nothing. `MbBatch.perfect` is never NULL: a world with no policies
points at an empty set that no queue can reach.

**Only the foe's tables are kept.** The reference computes a self side state
in `stateOf` and then indexes neither table by it, so `selfMoveOffsets` /
`selfMoveQF` are validated at load and dropped.

Sanity, 11-entrant / 4-seed field (2026-07-30): `jacco:perfect` won 40 of 40
against the field, where the same character on the tuned house AI won 33 of
40 and on `chaos` 32 of 40. Perfect dominating is the JS corpus's
expectation - a signal on the port, not a gate.

## System order

`app.yaml` `system_order`, executed by `mb_step_tick`:

| # | system | what it owns |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `match_phase` | intro / roundend / finisher / end, freeze, slowmo, shake decay; derives `MbClock.active` for the tick |
| 2 | `ai_decide` | bot roll + strategy decision for both sides (see the draw contract) |
| 3 | `step_fighter` | cooldowns, invuln, auto-face, gravity and landing, knockback slide, and the NON-attack state machine (stun + yank drag, block, dazed, jump + wall scramble, grounded incl. move start) |
| 4 | `step_attack` | the attack and grab_hold states: ripple tell, leaps, active-phase handlers (strike / grab / stance / chain yank / hazard spawns / retreat), move end |
| 5 | `step_hazards` | rats waves, coin zones, thrown objects; chip via the accumulating-division residual |
| 6 | `step_cross` | THE CROSS trigger, drain and window expiry |
| 7 | `step_finisher` | window arm, countdown, lapse and rearm |
| 8 | `separate` | overlap push |
| 9 | `announcements` | low-health threshold against `max_health` |
| 10 | `round_resolve` | timer expiry (integer cross-multiply), KO, double KO |
| 11 | `match_harvest` | host-side (`mb_world_harvest`): canonical result placement plus slot refill |

Every system self-gates on `MbClock.active` rather than being orchestrated
(root `CLAUDE.md` decision 15).

**The 3/4 split, and why `MbFsm.attack_deferred` exists.** The JS reference
runs one `stepFighter` per fighter that also dispatches the attack state.
Here those are two systems, so a fighter must be advanced by exactly one of
them per frame: `step_fighter` sets `attack_deferred` when it finds the
fighter ALREADY attacking (or holding a grab) at its entry and leaves the
whole per-frame tail (jump-edge latch, `t++`, ring clamp, retreat shuffle) to
`step_attack`. A fighter that STARTS a move inside `step_fighter` is finished
there, so a move's first frame is never stepped twice - the same frame timing
the reference produces.

## The simulator

```bash
./build/local/Debug/mb_sim.exe --stats --seeds 4
./build/local/Debug/mb_sim.exe --field "jacco:house,aistrop:chaos" --seeds 2
./build/local/Debug/mb_sim.exe --quiet --batch 1      # same hash, slower
./build/local/Release/mb_sim.exe --threads 20 --batch 1 --timing --quiet
./build/local/Release/mb_sim.exe --field "..." --json     # machine-readable
./build/local/Release/mb_sim.exe --backend cuda --quiet   # same rows, same hash
./build/local/Release/mb_sim.exe --version                # sim_version=mb-1
```

`--json` owns stdout: one object carrying `sim_version`, the entrant list, the
config actually used, `matches`/`ticks`/`finished`/`hash`, and every `MbResult`
field per row in canonical order. It is what `tools/mb_driver.py` parses -
nothing else is printed alongside it.

The default field is the roster crossed with `--bots` (`house,chaos,dummy`).
`--field` takes explicit entrant ids instead. One world holds **all** live
matches; a tick advances the whole batch by one frame. Slots are created once
and recycled in place, so the batch is a single archetype with stable columns
and there is no per-match archetype churn. Results are written to the slot
indexed by canonical (pairing, seed) position and printed in that order,
never in completion order - which is why the batch width is a pure
performance knob. `mb_sim --quiet` prints an FNV-1a hash of the result table;
**that hash must not change with `--batch`**, and `test_mb_sim` asserts it for
W in {1, 4, 16, 64}.

## Threading

`--threads N` (default `min(cores, 24)`) runs **N private worlds on N OS
threads**, one world per core, per architecture spec section 7.3. Flecs'
in-tick multithreaded pipeline is rejected there and `ecs_set_threads` is
never called: 11 systems x ~37.5k ticks would be ~413,000 sync barriers.
Threading lives entirely in `sim/mb_pool.c` - `mb_step.c` does not know
threads exist, and `mb_world.c` is unchanged apart from exposing
`mb_result_hash` over a bare result table.

**The partition (round robin).** Match `k` of the canonical queue goes to
world `k % N`. `--partition block` gives contiguous blocks instead and
exists only so the invariance test can prove the answer does not depend on
it. Round robin ships because the queue is pairing-major and match length is
bimodal *within* a pairing (a matchup that times out times out on every
seed), so contiguous blocks concentrate the 37.5k-frame matches on a few
threads: measured critical path at 24 threads over 2520 matches is 973,300
ticks round-robin vs 1,576,770 block, a 1.6x imbalance.

**Aggregation.** Each world writes results into per-match slots of one
shared array indexed by the **canonical** match index, so writes are
disjoint and no lock exists anywhere. The parent joins every thread before
reading, then prints rows and the hash in canonical order. Worlds are
created and destroyed serially on the parent (`ecs_init` touches flecs'
process-global bookkeeping); only `mb_world_run` - which touches nothing but
its own `MbBatch` and host arrays - runs concurrently. `--threads 1` creates
no thread at all and is byte-for-byte the pre-S4 single-world path.

**The `MbPerfectSet` is shared, never copied**: one pointer handed to every
world, freed by `main` after all worlds are destroyed.

**`ticks=` is not part of the determinism contract.** It is the longest
world's frame-tick count - the critical path - so it moves with `--threads`
and `--partition` by design. The oracle is the result rows and `hash=`.

### Measured tuning sweep (2026-07-30, 24-core Windows/MSVC Release)

2520 matches, 17,206,146 frame-steps (36 entrants x 4 seeds, perfect-backed).
Wall ms, `--timing` (stderr; never stdout, so the soak stays byte-stable):

| threads \ batch | 1 | 32 | 64 | 128 | 256 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **648** | 889 | 953 | 1052 | 1224 |
| 4 | 175 | 239 | 263 | 303 | 382 |
| 8 | 125 | 166 | 180 | 201 | 241 |
| 16 | 113 | 139 | 147 | 169 | 171 |
| 20 | **99-104** | - | - | - | - |
| 24 | 109-115 | 135 | 141 | 154 | 155 |

**Winning configuration: `--threads 20 --batch 1`, ~102 ms** (24 threads is
within 8%; batch 1-8 is within noise of each other). The 1200-match
no-perfect roster peaks earlier, at `--threads 8 --batch 1`, 56 ms - fewer
matches per world makes the single longest match the whole tail.

**The hash was identical at every one of the 50+ points measured**, which is
the spec 7.6 item 4 acceptance gate.

**Finding that corrects spec 7.3: larger W is monotonically WORSE here, not
better.** The section's model assumed ~300 ns of Flecs query setup per
system per tick, which large W amortises. This implementation binds the
component columns **once** at world creation and `mb_step_tick` is plain
array sweeps, so that constant is ~0 and there is nothing to amortise. What
large W does buy is (a) a bigger working set and (b) wasted iteration over
empty/finished slots during the drain tail. W therefore wants to be small on
CPU. It remains meaningful as the future CUDA launch width (S8), where the
launch overhead the model describes is real.

Versus the estimates: the spec's 40-90 ms at 24 cores was optimistic by
1.1-2.6x (measured 99-115 ms); the shortfall is scaling, not per-core speed -
24 threads buys 5.9x over 1 thread, of which round-robin balance accounts
for 0.74 and the rest is memory/SMT. Single-thread C is **37.6
ns/frame-step** against the JS reference's 342 ns [P], a **9.1x**
single-thread win (0.257 vs 2.34-2.67 ms/match); the full threaded run is
0.040 ms/match, **58-66x** the JS reference.

Shape of a 15-entrant / 4-seed run (420 matches, 2026-07-30): 41% / 45% /
14% side-0 wins / side-1 wins / draws, 85% of matches decided by at least one
KO, frames mean 8808, p50 3826, p95 37550, max 37558. The archived JS corpus
was mean 6849, p50 3425, p95 37550 - the same violently bimodal shape, which
is a sanity signal on the port, not a gate (`sim_version` is a new oracle).

## CUDA (stage S8)

`--backend cuda` runs the **same step functions** on the GPU.
`sim/mb_cuda.cu` includes `systems/native/mb_step.c` unchanged under `nvcc`
and replaces the host sweep loop with a kernel launch: one lane per match
slot, `--batch W` as the launch width.

```bash
./build/local/Release/mb_sim.exe --backend cuda --field "..." --seeds 4 --quiet
./build/local/Release/mb_sim.exe --backend cuda --batch 512 --cuda-frames 256 --timing
```

**Optional in CMake, loud when present.** `check_language(CUDA)` builds
`mb_cuda.cu` when a toolkit is found and `sim/mb_cuda_none.c` when it is not;
the latter's `mb_cuda_run` **refuses** rather than falling back to the CPU
(root `CLAUDE.md` decision 12). A CUDA box that fails to compile the backend
fails the build. `test_mb_cuda` SKIPs when no device is present. Architecture
is `MB_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES` (default `native`).

**Two knobs, both pure performance.** `--batch W` is the launch width
(default: the whole queue, because on a device the width IS the parallelism);
`--cuda-frames N` is how many frames one launch steps (default 1024), which
amortizes the ~5 us launch overhead over ~37,600 frames. Neither moves a row -
`test_mb_cuda` asserts both.

**Scheduling is on the device.** Harvest and refill run in the same kernel as
the frame, so there is no per-tick host round trip. An idle lane claims the
next queue entry with an `atomicAdd`, so unlike `mb_world_fill` the
slot-to-pairing assignment is not fixed - and cannot matter, because matches
are independent and results are written at their canonical index. That is the
same invariant that makes the CPU batch width a performance knob.

**One kernel per frame, not per system.** A slot's ten `mb_k_*` calls touch
only that slot, so running them back to back inside one lane is identical to
ten launches; for the same reason a lane may run N frames before the grid
resyncs. There is no cross-slot data flow anywhere in `mb_step.c`.

**The shared tables are uploaded once per run**, never per match or per launch
(spec section 7.2): `MbRules` as a flat copy, and the `MbPerfectSet` as one
device blob per solved pair with `cdf` / `vs_committed` re-pointed into it.

**`MB_FN` is `__device__ static` on CUDA, and the `static` is load-bearing.**
nvcc emits a HOST-side stub for every non-static `__device__` function under
that function's own external name; with `mb_core` in the same executable the
linker can bind the CPU backend's callers to the stub, and the CPU backend
then silently stops simulating. Measured, not theorised - `--backend cpu`
failed exactly that way the first time `mb_cuda.cu` linked. Internal linkage
keeps the names out of the object entirely.

### Measured (2026-07-30, RTX 5090, CUDA 13.3, Release)

Full 36-entrant / 4-seed perfect-backed field: **2520 matches, 22,719,009
frame-steps, 37,605 ticks**.

| backend | configuration | wall |
|---|---|---|
| CPU | `--threads 20 --batch 1` | **105 ms** |
| CUDA | `--batch 2520 --cuda-frames 1024` | **577 ms** (485 ms kernel, 4 ms upload, ~88 ms CUDA context creation) |
| CUDA | `--cuda-frames 1` | 2720 ms (one launch per frame) |
| CUDA | `--batch 512` | 1212 ms |
| CUDA | `--batch 128` | 3403 ms |

**The CPU wins by 5.5x, which is the spec's own prediction** (section 3.3
estimated a CUDA wall of 190 ms - 1.5 s, central 490 ms; measured 485 ms of
kernel). The reason is section 3.3's, unchanged: 2520 lanes is 12% of the
5090's lanes and 0.7% of its warp slots, so there is no second warp to hide
latency, and 90% of warps contain at least one match that runs the full
~37,600 frames. Wider is monotonically better here (the opposite of the CPU,
where W=1 wins) because W IS the parallelism; the whole queue resident is the
right default and smaller widths only lengthen the tick count.

Where the time goes: 37,605 frames x 2520 lanes = **94.8 M lane-frames for
22.7 M useful frame-steps** - 76% of the device's work is finished or empty
slots waiting out the longest match. That is the section 3.4 crossover
restated: nothing here is fixable by better GPU engineering, only by having
more independent matches to fill the machine.

## The Python driver (`tools/mb_driver.py`)

The orchestration layer's first brick, and the thing S5 (grammar scorer, patch
emitter, loop/search) will import rather than shelling out ad hoc. It is a
**driver, not a scorer**: it counts W/L/D, head-to-head, draws, timeouts and
finishers, and stops there.

```bash
# CLI
python3 sandboxes/monkey-baiting/tools/mb_driver.py \
    --roster "jacco:house,brock:chaos,billy:dummy" --seeds 4 \
    --out runs/run-001.json [--verify] [--perfect DIR] [--threads N]

# library (S5)
from mb_driver import RunConfig, run_tournament
run = run_tournament(RunConfig(roster=[...], seeds=4), verify=True)
```

`--roster` takes a comma list, or a path to a roster file: a plain
`- <char>:<bot>` list always works (stdlib only), and the mapping form
(`roster:` plus optional `seeds`/`perfect`/`sandbox`/`batch`/`partition`)
needs PyYAML and is refused with that reason when it is absent.

Three seams, deliberately separate: `build_argv` (invocation), `run_sim`
(invoke + parse), `build_record` (the archived JSON).

**Pinned configuration.** Every flag that could move a number is passed
explicitly - `--threads` above all, because the binary's default is
machine-dependent. The auto policy is `min(cores, 20)`, dropping to
`min(8, cores)` below 1500 queued matches (the measured S4 result); `--threads`
overrides it, and either way the mode, rule, core count and chosen number land
in the record.

**The run record** (`schema: mb-run-record/1`) holds `sim_version`, the binary
path/mtime/size, the full config incl. the exact argv and cwd, the thread
policy, the queue shape, `oracle` (**hash + rows, the whole oracle**),
`non_oracle` (`ticks`, wall ms - `ticks` moves with `--threads`/`--partition`
by design), `finished`, the aggregate counts, and the `--verify` block.

**`--verify`** runs the same invocation twice and requires identical rows and
hash - never ticks. A mismatch exits 4.

**Failures are typed and never silent** (root `CLAUDE.md` decision 12):
`SimRefusal` for `mb_sim` exit 2 (CLI exit 2), carrying the stderr line that
caused it; `SimCrash` for any other nonzero (CLI exit 3) with captured stderr;
`BinaryMissing` naming the cmake target; `SimOutputError` when the result table
does not match the canonical queue the driver independently enumerated.

### `sim_version` - the oracle stamp

`MB_SIM_VERSION` in `systems/native/mb_types.h` is the **single source**.
`mb_sim --version` prints it, `mb_sim --json` embeds it, and the driver copies
it into every record. **Bump it for any change that can move a result row** -
the draw contract, a step function, an authored constant in `app.yaml` or
`data/`, entrant identity or seeding, the perfect-policy bucketing or CDF math.
Results under different `sim_version`s are not comparable and are never scored
against each other; champions get re-scored (architecture spec ruling 5(a)).
Performance-only changes (threads, batch, partition, and the CUDA backend and
its two knobs) must NOT bump it - a compile target cannot move a row, and a
row that moves is a bug to fix, never a baseline to re-cut.

## Tests

```bash
cd build/local && ctest -R "test_mb_sim|test_mb_cuda|test_mb_engine|test_mb_driver|test_mb_loop" \
    --output-on-failure -C Debug
```

`test_mb_cuda` (6 tests) is the S8 acceptance gate: it runs BOTH backends over
one queue in one process and compares the `MbResult` rows field by field plus
the FNV-1a hash - for a single-bot field, for a mixed-bot field (every
strategy branch, so every draw site), and for a `perfect` entrant against the
checked-in tiny policy fixture. Then the invariance three: launch width,
frames per launch, and the same command twice. It SKIPs when no CUDA device is
present, which is also what a build with no CUDA toolkit reports.

`test_mb_engine` (12 tests, links `engine_lib`) covers engine hosting: the
sandbox lifecycle building a hosted world, the slots being first-class engine
entities that `GET /api/entities` returns, one turn being exactly one frame,
**the hosted match producing the same result row and result hash as the
`mb_sim` path in the same number of ticks**, determinism across two engines,
named configs and the refusal of an unknown one, the `MbMatchRequest` spawn +
`MbSpawnRequest` value path, an injected match ALSO matching its `mb_sim` row,
a refused injection being consumed rather than repeated, and a second
`app/start` rebuilding the batch rather than accumulating one.

32 tests: the rules load and its refusal to default; the JS literals now
being authored data with their exact Q16.16 conversions; the C schema against
the on-disk component YAML in BOTH directions (the drift guard); the
character/move tables against the real data; precomputed launch sin/cos; the
data-driven cap assertions; bot table loading and validation; entrant
identity resolution and every one of its refusals; the accumulating-division
residual's exactness after N frames; the world build and fighter indexing;
canonical result order; a real match reaching the fight phase and finishing;
non-triviality (KOs happen, both sides win, frame counts vary); repeat
determinism; and batch-width invariance. Then perfect-policy playback: the
loader's derived strides against a hand-built tiny fixture
(`tests/fixtures/policy-tiny`, small enough that every number is checkable by
hand), the u32 CDF's exact boundaries and its saturation, selection
determinism at those boundaries, every refusal (stale encoding, truncated
array, unsolved pair, absent directory, and a queue needing an unbacked
pair), the bucketing and side-state index math asserted directly against
hand-placed fighters, and batch-width invariance with a perfect entrant on
both sides. The last of those needs a real solved artefact and SKIPS when
the policies are not on the box; everything else runs from the checked-in
fixture. Finally the stage-4 threading gates: thread-count invariance
(1/4/8), `--threads 1` being tick-for-tick the old single-world path,
partition invariance (round-robin vs block, incl. a thread count that does
not divide the queue), threads x batch independence, and two that drive the
BINARY - a determinism soak (the same command line three times, stdout
compared byte for byte) and the `hash=` token across `--threads 1/4/8`.
Those two SKIP when `mb_sim` is not where a standard build puts it.

`test_mb_loop` (89 tests, stdlib Python, no pip deps) covers the balance loop:
the YAML reader's subset and its refusals, canonical loading and overlay
merge, the static frame analysis, roster resolution, tournament aggregation
and the issue vocabulary, grammar validation and every detector, SCORING
DETERMINISM, the legal-patch verifier (pins, envelopes, schema, layer), seeded
emission (same seed -> byte-identical candidate directories), the per-pair
model-hash gate, the model exporter (the JS-semantics levers byte-identity
rests on, a two-character fixture's YAML becoming a consistent model, the
`MB_DATA_DIR` staging override moving exactly the pairs it should, export
reproducibility and what the hash covers), staging, the comparability tuple, the archive round trip
including `verify` catching a non-strict accept, and the depth-first search's
control flow against a stubbed evaluator. The three tests that drive `mb_sim`
SKIP when it is absent.

`test_mb_driver` (stdlib Python, no pip deps; ctest passes it the freshly built
`mb_sim` and it SKIPs when that binary is absent) covers the driver: the
canonical queue enumeration, the thread policy incl. its 1500-match boundary,
both roster formats and their refusals, every pinned flag being present in the
argv, the run-record schema, hash and row stability across two separate driver
runs, the aggregate's counting, and the failure paths - a bogus `--perfect`
directory and a perfect entrant with no policies both arriving as `SimRefusal`
with the causing stderr line (CLI exit 2), and a missing binary naming the
cmake build.

## The balance loop (`tools/mb_loop/`)

Architecture stage S5. The thing the freeze was waiting on: a scorer, an
explorer and an archive, in Python, on top of `mb_sim` (through
`mb_driver.py`) and the Python solver. Design contract:
`web/monkey-baiting/docs/balance-loop.md`.

```bash
# score the committed state (no patches, no solver)
python3 -m mb_loop score --roster no-perfect --seeds 4

# score against the solved yardstick, and validate the ledger's citations
python3 -m mb_loop score --roster full --perfect web/monkey-baiting/policies-full-v3 \
    --ledger web/monkey-baiting/docs/balance.md

# one accept/reject run, cheap: no solver in the loop at all
python3 -m mb_loop run --seed 21 --roster no-perfect \
    --max-depth 4 --candidates-per-depth 6

# one run against the solved roster, re-solving what the gate says changed
python3 -m mb_loop run --seed 21 --roster full --solve-profile coarse \
    --backend cuda --max-depth 2 --candidates-per-depth 2

# RNG-varied runs until run_patience consecutive runs improve nothing
python3 -m mb_loop search --seed 100 --roster full --solve-profile coarse

# inspect / re-score a stored run
python3 -m mb_loop archive list
python3 -m mb_loop archive verify runs/run-21-...
python3 -m mb_loop archive rescore runs/run-21-... --grammar data/balance-grammar.yaml
```

Run it **from the project root** (`mb_sim` and the exporter resolve paths
relative to the working directory), with `sandboxes/monkey-baiting/tools` on
`PYTHONPATH`.

**Modules**, bottom up: `yamlio` (the YAML subset reader/writer), `data`
(canonical load, overlay merge, path resolution), `framedata` (static frame
analysis -- class envelopes and dominance, no simulation), `tournament`
(roster + `mb_sim` + the grammar-shaped report), `grammar` (validation,
detection, the score), `patch` (pins + envelopes as the legal-patch verifier),
`emit` (the seeded emitter), `stage` (materialising a candidate on disk),
`model_export` (the solver model exporter: YAML -> discretized Markov-game
model), `solve` (export, the per-pair hash gate, cold solve), `archive` (the run
archive and the equal-height champion archive), `search` (depth-first
accept/reject), `runner`/`cli`.

**The score.** Sum of weight x detected signals over the grammar's scorable
situations. **LOWER IS BETTER**; zero means every band holds. A candidate is
accepted only on **strict improvement** -- equal rejects, so the loop cannot
wander a plateau. Base score on the committed data, grammar v3, `sim_version`
mb-1: **93** on the 31-entrant no-perfect roster, **115** on the 36-entrant
full roster (2026-07-30).

**Scores are new.** They come from this simulator under its own `sim_version`
oracle and are never compared with anything the JS stack produced (ruling
5(a)). What carried over is the aggregation and the issue vocabulary, so the
grammar still measures what the playbook reacts to.

**No new telemetry was needed.** Nine of grammar v3's twelve situations are
fed by outcome aggregates that are already columns of an `mb_sim --json`
result row (win/loss/draw, rounds, timeouts, capped, drawn); one is static
math over the YAML; two are manual by design. `mb_sim` is unchanged and
`sim_version` stays `mb-1`.

### The staging seam -- the thing that makes a candidate real

`mb_sim` loads `<sandbox>/app.yaml` and `<sandbox>/data/**` **from disk**, and
so does the model exporter. A candidate held only as an in-memory overlay is
therefore invisible to both: the simulator would score the committed data and
every round would come back score-neutral. `stage.py` materialises each
candidate into a scratch sandbox under `<run>/stage/d<depth>-v<visit>-<id>/`
and both tools are pointed at it.

The staged bytes are produced by the same **textual leaf replacement**
`--apply` uses -- never by re-serialising a parsed document -- so the state
that gets scored is byte-identical to the state a later `--apply` would
commit, comments and formatting intact.

### Solve orchestration and the re-solve gate

A perfect policy is only valid for the tuning it was solved against, so a
candidate that moves character data must be re-solved before it can be
scored against the perfect entrants. Per candidate: stage -> export
(`mb_loop.model_export`, in-process, pointed at the stage via `data_dir`) ->
per-pair SHA-256 gate -> selective cold solve.

**The exporter is a PORT, and byte-identity is its contract.** It replaced
`node test/solver-export.js` and produces the same manifest bytes and the same
`.bin` bytes, therefore the same `model_sha`, for every pair -- verified on the
whole cast at both the default (gap 6 / fq 3, 345 files / 1033 MB) and `coarse`
(gap 12 / fq 6) resolutions. That is not tidiness: run-1 archives store
`model_sha` values, and a byte-divergent exporter would silently invalidate
every stored per-pair gate decision. `_js_round`, `_js_json` and the array
typecodes in `model_export.py` exist for exactly that, and `test_mb_loop`
asserts each of them directly. It costs about 2.2x node's wall (whole cast at
gap 6 / fq 3: 61 s vs 28 s; `coarse`: 23 s vs 10 s), which the per-pair gate
absorbs -- most candidates re-solve a third of the cast.

The gate is **model_sha AND solver_version AND solver_backend**. The solver
consumes the exported model, not the YAML, so the exact criterion is "did this
pair's exported model change?" -- measured: a single-character edit moves
**5 of 15** pair models, and the other 10 policy sets are copied. An unknown
version never compares equal, so the gate always fails closed.

Solves are **cold, always** (no `--resume` anywhere): warm starts tie-break
differently and may never feed a score. CUDA is the default backend and is
legitimate under the amended cold-solve rule -- cold, the backend tuple
recorded per policy, and `solve_cuda.py`'s canary (on by default) as the
reproducibility gate. Measured on this box: a whole-cast coarse solve is
**~23 s export + ~22 s solve** (the export was ~9 s under the node exporter),
and two identical solves produced identical policy hashes.

`run.json.solve` and each `score.json.lineage` carry everything a later
comparison needs: profile, backend, `solver_version`, `solver_backend`,
`policy_sha`, per-pair `model_sha`, `resolved_pairs`, `reused_pairs` and
`exporter_version` (the interpreter: export bytes are deterministic within one
machine + one interpreter, which is the comparability scope of a `model_sha`).

### The archive

`<runs-root>/<run-id>/` holds `run.json` (`mb-run/2`), the `grammar.yaml` and
`pins.yaml` the run was scored under, `base/` and `rounds/NNNN/` (each with
`candidate.json`, the patch overlay, `tournament.json`, `framedata.json` and
`score.json`), and `final/` (`stack.json`, the merged overlay, `ledger.md`).
The schemas are **fresh** -- no compatibility with the node stack's archives
is claimed or wanted.

`<runs-root>/archive.json` is the **equal-height champion archive**: the best
known stack at each height. A height-N state survives only if it OUTPERFORMS
the archived champion -- equal never survives. Champions are offered as
accepts happen, not from the surviving stack, because a later backout does
not un-discover that a height-N state scored what it scored.

**Comparability is never assumed.** Every champion carries the tuple two
scores must share to be compared at all: `sim_version`, grammar version and
hash, pins hash, roster mode and size, eval seeds, solve profile, solver
version and solver backend. An archive written under a different tuple is not
consulted rather than silently ranked against.

`archive rescore` re-runs the analysis over each round's STORED detector
inputs under a different grammar -- no matches, no solves, never writes.

### Reproducibility

`--pin-clock ISO8601` pins every wall-clock stamp the archive carries (run id,
`started_at`, `finished_at`, each patch's `emitted_at`), which makes the
archive a pure function of its inputs. Two runs of the same inputs into the
same path then produce byte-identical artifacts. It changes nothing else: no
score, no seed, no ordering.

### What the loop must never do

- **Never edit `data/` during a run.** Every evaluation is an overlay plus a
  stage; the canonical files are written only by an explicit `--apply`.
- **Never touch a pinned parameter.** 47 pins are enforced by the verifier
  before any solve or evaluation, and a pinned target is not even emitted.
- **Never compare across `sim_version`, grammar version, roster or solver
  tuple.** Re-score the champions instead.
- **Never mix pair sets in one archive entry.** A cast solve's trajectory
  depends on which pairs were solved together (`3-cuda-cast`), which is why
  `resolved_pairs` is recorded per lineage entry.

## Engine hosting (stage S6)

The same game, inside the engine, as an ordinary sandbox.

```bash
./build/local/Debug/flecs-ecs.exe                       # from the project root
curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/sandbox/select -d '{"name":"monkey-baiting"}'
curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/app/start               # or {"config":"quad"}
curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/sim/turn                # one frame
curl localhost:8080/api/entities                        # MbMatch0, MbFighter0_0/1
curl localhost:8080/api/entities/MbFighter0_0           # every component
```

**FRAME = TURN.** `app.yaml` `system_order` is the frame order, registered by
`monkey_baiting_register_native_systems` as eleven native systems: the ten
`mb_step_*` sweeps of `mb_step_tick` plus `match_harvest`, which is
`mb_world_run`'s tail (harvest, admit injections, refill) done one turn at a
time. So one `POST /api/sim/turn` advances every live match by exactly one
frame at `logic_hz`, and the engine's turn counter IS the frame counter.
`app/start` deals the first match before the first turn, exactly as
`mb_world_run` fills before its first tick - which is what keeps the two paths
frame-aligned.

**Batch vs interactive.** `mb_sim` runs a whole field in one world: a canonical
queue, `sim.batch` slots, results at queue indices. Hosting is that same
machinery in the small - `app.yaml`'s `engine:` block gives the batch width
(1 by default) and the one match queued at start, and the queue is then fed
incrementally instead of up front. Batch width remains a pure performance knob
in both: it does not move a result row.

**Injection (the repo's ECS interaction pattern).** `MbSpawnRequest` on an
injected entity appends a match to the queue; `match_harvest` drains it,
because queue admission is host-side scheduling, not simulation. From the CLI:

```bash
python3 tools/inject.py --template MbMatchRequest
python3 tools/inject.py --entity MbMatchRequest_1 --set \
  'MbSpawnRequest: {char0: "billy", bot0: "house", char1: "puss", bot1: "chaos", seed_index: 3}'
curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/sim/turn      # admitted here
```

Two calls, not one: `POST /api/entities/spawn/:template` hands its raw JSON
body to an override parser that reads YAML only, so the values land through
`PUT /api/entities/:id`. That is a generic engine limitation, not this
sandbox's. The four fields carry the two halves of each entrant id separately
for the same reason - `tools/inject.py`'s `--set` mini-parser cannot express a
value containing a colon. `mb_engine.c` composes `"<char>:<bot>"` and resolves
it through `mb_rules_resolve_entrant`, so entrant identity and seeding are
unchanged.

**No file statics.** The host hangs off the ecs world's user context
(`ecs_set_ctx`), never a file static, so a world-per-core simulator and an
engine host coexist in one process (rule 1 above). `mb_world_attach` /
`mb_world_attach_bind` build the same batch on a world the engine owns;
`mb_world_create` is that pair on its own world, so `mb_sim` is byte-for-byte
unchanged.

**What hosting must never do is fork behaviour.** `test_mb_engine` asserts that
the engine-hosted match and the `mb_sim` path produce the SAME result row and
the same result hash for the same pairing and seed, and that the engine needed
exactly as many turns as `mb_world_run` needed ticks. Engine integration is
not a reason to bump `sim_version`; if a row moves, something is wrong.

**Not in the pipeline.** `monkey_baiting_register_native_systems` refuses when
`engine->pipeline_active` (any `--threads`). Eleven systems that each sweep
the whole batch have nothing to gain from Flecs' in-tick pipeline, and this
game's parallelism is world-per-core (`mb_pool`), never in-tick.

**Turn caps and app.yaml quirks.** `max_turns: 0` - there is no engine-level
cap, match length is bounded by the rules. `templates:` must use the inline
`[A, B]` form: the engine's app.yaml reader accepts nothing else there, and
entity templates are not auto-swept from disk the way components are, so a
block list means the prefabs silently never load.

### The engine surface in `data/apps.yaml`

`./tools/run.py monkey-baiting --rest` is a smoke path over REST. Direct mode
drives a CONSOLE client and this sandbox has none - S7 shipped a browser
client, not a console one; the headless batch runner is `mb_sim` /
`tools/mb_driver.py`, which is a different and much faster thing. run.py's
fixed 1000-turn ceiling is ~16 s of game time, so it normally ends on TURN
LIMIT.

## The WASM client (stage S7)

The shipped game, on the C core. `clients/wasm/` is three files and a hard
rule: **the shell computes no gameplay.** Every number it draws came out of a
`wasm_*` export. The retired `web/monkey-baiting/logic.js` has no successor
here (architecture ruling 1.4(b)).

| file | what |
|---|---|
| `wasm_main.c` | the export surface. Guards its `<emscripten.h>` include, so ctest builds it NATIVELY into `test_mb_wasm` |
| `shell.js` | presentation only: one rAF loop, canvas drawing, keys, screen flow |
| `chars.js` | the retired game's vector character art, taken across unchanged - it is presentation-pure (`draw(ctx, id, spec, palette)`) |

No `clients/lib/` split: the repo's browser-client rendering policy is for
sandboxes with two or more client surfaces. A second client is the trigger to
extract, not before.

```bash
./tools/build-wasm.sh monkey-baiting                    # build/wasm-monkey-baiting/
./tools/localhost.sh monkey-baiting --client wasm       # local playtest
python3 tools/publish.py monkey-baiting/wasm            # -> kitaekatt.github.io/games/monkey-baiting/
```

**Scalar accessors, raw fixed point** (root `CLAUDE.md` decision 19). Nothing
returns a pointer for JS to walk with `HEAP32`. Positions, velocities, health
and hurtbox sizes cross the boundary as raw Q16.16 with `wasm_fp_one()`
alongside; the shell divides once, at draw time, and never feeds a converted
value back. Truncating to whole pixels would lose the sub-pixel motion the
fight animates on; a double would put a float in the path of integer state.

**Presentation reads authored data.** The cast, ring geometry, round
structure, per-character max health, which move keys a character authors, the
current move's startup/active/recovery split, and each character's `palette:`
all come from the core. The palette is not part of `MbChar` - the simulation
never sees a colour - but it IS authored in `data/characters/<name>.yaml`, so
`wasm_char_palette` reads it from the same preloaded bundle the rules came
from rather than letting the shell keep a second copy that could drift.

### `human_input` - the twelfth system

A browser player overrides one fighter's input. That is HOST work, so
`mb_engine.c` registers a twelfth system, `human_input`, between `ai_decide`
and `step_fighter` in `app.yaml`'s `system_order`. It overwrites the input
`ai_decide` just wrote for a side the client has claimed
(`mb_engine_set_human` / `mb_engine_set_human_input`, both per-host, never a
file static - rule 1).

The bot still decides and is simply overruled. Keeping `ai_decide` in the
frame costs one wasted decision and leaves the frame order, the system list
and the draw contract identical to the batch path, which is what keeps "the
engine host and `mb_sim` agree" a checkable statement. With no side claimed
the system reads an empty mask and returns.

**It does not bump `sim_version`.** It cannot move a result row: inert in
every batch run, no step function changed, no authored constant moved.
`test_mb_sim`, `test_mb_engine` and `test_mb_loop_acceptance` passing
unchanged is the evidence.

### Picking a fight

`wasm_start_match(char0, bot0, char1, bot1, seed_index)` composes the
canonical `"<char>:<bot>"` entrant ids and calls `mb_engine_restart`, which is
the teardown-and-rebuild a second `app/start` already did, with the pairing as
a parameter instead of an `app.yaml` lookup. Identity and seeding are
unchanged; `mb_rules_resolve_entrant` is still the only resolver. Batch width
still comes from `app.yaml` - a harness knob, not a client parameter (root
`CLAUDE.md` decision 13).

A human-played side wears the `dummy` bot id, because identity and seeding are
derived from the id string and a nameless side would need a second identity
scheme. **A result row from a human-played match is therefore labelled
`dummy`; such rows are not tournament rows and are never scored.**

### What shipped, what is deferred

Shipped: the booth stage, both fighters in authored colours with the
reference's pose/phase mapping, all three hazard kinds, chalk slates with
health and round pips, the watch, round number, THE CROSS / FINISH HIM
banners, screen shake, keyboard plus a seven-button on-screen pad, and a
character-select screen driven by the core's roster. The campaign is an
**arcade ladder**: pick a monkey, fight the rest of the cast in roster order.

Deferred: the five **scripted campaigns**. Their narrative data
(`web/monkey-baiting/data/structure.yaml` and `script.yaml` - a node graph of
screens, announcer lines, bill-posters, continue screens and endings) was
never ported to `data/` because it is not gameplay and the balance loop never
touched it. Also deferred: audio and the announcer, the betting/odds
presentation, and the finisher cinematic's bespoke framing. None is blocked -
they are shell work over exports that already exist.

### Tests, and what is still outstanding

`test_mb_wasm` (13 tests) drives the export surface natively: every accessor
total before init, after free and out of range; ring/round/cast/move-authorship
against an independently loaded `MbRules`; palettes against the authored YAML;
refusal of an unknown character or bot; the human seam overriding the bot,
moving the fighter both ways, and clearing latched buttons on release; and
**the determinism gate** - a whole match played through `wasm_start_match` /
`wasm_tick` produces the row `mb_world_run` produces for that pairing and
seed, twice running, with a different `seed_index` producing a different
fight.

**Outstanding: `emcc` is not installed on this box.** The WASM binary has not
been built, served or played. Everything short of the compile is done. The
browser half of the cross-target gate runs from the console once a build
exists: `MB.dev.exhibition(char0, bot0, char1, bot1, seed)` plays a bot-vs-bot
match headlessly and `MB.dev.probe()` reports the row and hash to compare
against `mb_sim`.
name: monkey-baiting
description: "MONKEY BAITING - fighting-game sandbox and batch tournament simulator"

# The engine sandbox surface. map_size mirrors the game's internal render
# resolution (data/tuning.yaml internal_width/internal_height in the JS
# reference); the fight itself is played in Q16.16 pixel space, not on a grid.
map_size:
  x: 400
  y: 225
# No engine-level turn cap: a turn is one 60Hz frame (see `engine:` below) and
# match length is bounded by the rules (rounds.time_seconds and
# rounds.max_rounds_per_match), not by a turn count.
max_turns: 0

# MapSize is avk's component; the engine creates the Map entity from map_size
# above and needs it registered (the rt-demo precedent).
imports:
  avk:
    components: [MapSize]

# INLINE LISTS ARE REQUIRED. The engine's app.yaml reader (parse_yaml_list in
# engine/src/engine/app.c) accepts only the `[A, B]` form here; a block dash
# list parses as zero templates, and entity templates are not auto-swept from
# disk the way components are - so the prefabs would silently never load.
templates:
  components: [MbBody, MbVitals, MbFsm, MbMoveSlot, MbCooldowns, MbRetreat, MbGrab, MbYank, MbRefs, MbBrain, MbInput, MbFighter, MbClock, MbRng, MbPair, MbCross, MbFinisher, MbOutcome, MbHazards, MbMatch, MbSpawnRequest]
  entities: [MbFighterUnit, MbMatchSlot, MbMatchRequest]

# Frame order per docs/specs/tournament-simulator-architecture.md section 4.3.
# The tournament simulator (mb_sim) runs these same step functions in the same
# order without the engine; see systems/native/mb_step.c.
# `human_input` is the one entry with no counterpart in mb_step_tick: it is the
# interactive seam (systems/native/mb_engine.h), and it overwrites the input
# ai_decide just wrote for a side a browser client has claimed. With no human
# side registered - every mb_sim run, every test that compares against one - it
# reads an empty mask and returns, so it cannot move a result row and does not
# bump sim_version.
system_order:
  - match_phase
  - ai_decide
  - human_input
  - step_fighter
  - step_attack
  - step_hazards
  - step_cross
  - step_finisher
  - separate
  - announcements
  - round_resolve
  - match_harvest

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENGINE HOSTING (architecture stage S6). What POST /api/app/start builds.
#
# FRAME = TURN: the eleven systems above are the ten mb_step_* sweeps of
# mb_step_tick plus the host-side harvest, so one POST /api/sim/turn advances
# every live match by exactly one frame at logic_hz.
#
# The tournament simulator runs a whole FIELD in one world - a canonical queue
# of (pairing, seed) matches, `sim.batch` slots wide, results placed at queue
# indices. Engine hosting is that same machinery in the small: `batch` slots,
# one match queued here at start, and more admitted at run time by injecting
# MbSpawnRequest entities (tools/inject.py). Batch width is a harness knob in
# both, never a game-affecting parameter - the result rows do not move with it.
#
# Every key is required. A named config (POST /api/app/start {"config":"..."})
# reads engine.configs.<name> and must declare all four itself: a named block
# does not inherit from this one (root CLAUDE.md decision 12).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
engine:
  batch: 1
  entrant0: "jacco:house"
  entrant1: "brock:chaos"
  seed_index: 0
  configs:
    duel:
      batch: 1
      entrant0: "jacco:house"
      entrant1: "brock:chaos"
      seed_index: 0
    quad:
      batch: 4
      entrant0: "billy:house"
      entrant1: "puss:chaos"
      seed_index: 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rules consumed by the simulator (mb_rules_load). Ported verbatim from the JS
# reference: web/monkey-baiting/data/tuning.yaml and data/structure.yaml.
# Every key below is REQUIRED - a missing key is a hard load failure, never a
# default (root CLAUDE.md decision 12, "no silent fallbacks").
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
logic_hz: 60

# The cast, in canonical order. A char_id is an index into this list, so the
# order is part of the determinism contract - append, never reorder. Each
# name resolves to data/characters/<name>.yaml.
characters: [jacco, brock, billy, puss, aistrop]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCALING. Every fractional value in this file and in data/characters/*.yaml is
# authored as a decimal string and converted ONCE, at load, to Q16.16 signed
# fixed point (one unit = 65536) by exact integer arithmetic in
# systems/native/mb_rules.c (mb_parse_fp - no strtod, no float in the
# pipeline). Whole-frame counters, pixel bounds, indices and counts stay plain
# integers. Probabilities are additionally pre-scaled to u32 thresholds over
# 2^32 and compared against a raw RNG word, so the hot path never divides.
# See components/README.md for the full contract.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ring:
  left: 26
  right: 374
  center: 200
  start_offset: 70          # px either side of centre a fighter starts on

combat:
  block_chip_ratio: 0.2
  low_health_announce: 0.25
  knockback_scale: 1.0
  ko_slowmo_frames: 70
  ko_fall_frames: 100

  # The KO sprawl. These were bare literals in the JS reference
  # (logic.js koFall: vy = -3.2, vx = -facing * 2.2).
  ko_fall_vy: -3.2          # px/frame, negative = upward
  ko_fall_vx: 2.2           # px/frame, applied away from the facing direction

  # Horizontal velocity decay per frame. Two different values in the
  # reference: 0.85 during the round-end lull (stepPhysicsOnly) and 0.86
  # during a knockback slide (stepFighter).
  idle_vx_decay: 0.85
  stun_vx_decay: 0.86

  # Height epsilons. ground_epsilon is the "grounded" test (|y| and vy);
  # air_epsilon is the nudge that puts a fighter off the floor when a jump,
  # launch or KO begins.
  ground_epsilon: 0.001
  air_epsilon: 0.01

  # Knockback shaping (logic.js resolveHit).
  knockback_vy_scale: 1.3   # vertical component multiplier
  launch_vy_threshold: 1.4  # below this the hit does not lift a grounded foe
  pushback_hit_scale: 0.3
  pushback_block_scale: 0.4

  # Hit-freeze floors by outcome.
  block_freeze: 2
  armor_freeze: 3

  # Brock's Barrel Set (stance) counter-hit.
  stance_freeze: 6
  stance_attacker_stun: 18
  stance_shake: 3.0
  stance_attacker_vx: 2.5

  # Overlap separation: fighters are pushed apart when their half-widths
  # overlap by more than separate_inset and their heights are within
  # separate_y_window of each other.
  separate_inset: 4.0
  separate_y_window: 20.0

  # Offsets, in px past the grabber's half-width, at which a grabbed fighter
  # is planted and a yanked fighter is dragged to.
  grab_offset: 4.0
  yank_offset: 10.0
  yank_arrive_epsilon: 2.0

  # Walk-speed multipliers for the airborne drift and the post-exchange
  # retreat shuffle; the wall-scramble jump's fraction of jump_velocity and
  # the px margin that counts as "at the wall".
  walk_air_ratio: 0.8
  walk_retreat_ratio: 0.7
  wall_jump_ratio: 0.9
  wall_margin: 2.0

  # STAKES DOWN: the step-out toward the ring edge.
  retreat_step: 2.5
  retreat_epsilon: 2.0
  retreat_invuln_frames: 2

  # Hazards. coins_margin is the px inset a coin zone's centre is drawn from
  # inside each ring wall; thrown objects fall from thrown_spawn_y at
  # thrown_gravity px/frame^2 and land within thrown_hit_radius of a fighter,
  # scattered thrown_spread px around the target.
  coins_margin: 20
  thrown_spawn_y: -140.0
  thrown_gravity: 0.3
  thrown_hit_radius: 16.0
  thrown_spread: 60

  # Breaking THE CROSS.
  cross_break_freeze: 8
  cross_break_shake: 4.0
  cross_break_vx: 2.0

rounds:
  wins_to_take_match: 2
  time_seconds: 120
  intro_frames: 170
  end_frames: 140
  max_rounds_per_match: 5

finisher:
  health_threshold: 0.22
  window_seconds: 6.0
  rearm_frames: 240
  cinematic_frames: 240

ai:
  decision_interval_frames: 16

  # Shape constants of the `default` strategy. Per-character aggression,
  # ranges and chances live in each character's own `ai:` block; these are the
  # multipliers and margins the strategy applies on top of them, and were bare
  # literals in the JS reference (logic.js defaultPolicy).
  special_roll_ratio: 0.45     # P(special) = this * aggression
  heavy_roll_ratio: 0.35       # P(heavy)   = this * aggression
  yank_dodge_chance: 0.6       # jump out of a winding chain yank
  yank_use_chance: 0.5         # fire the yank at a grounded foe at range
  stakes_down_roll_chance: 0.5 # step out of the ring when pressed
  block_range_bonus: 26.0      # block reacts within poke_range + this
  stance_range: 50.0           # answer pressure with a stance inside this gap
  stakes_down_range: 46.0
  yank_min_range: 55.0
  heavy_range_bonus: 12.0
  keep_range_margin: 8.0
  poke_margin: 6.0

# Batch defaults for the tournament simulator. Overridable on the mb_sim
# command line, which is a harness parameter (batch width), never a
# game-affecting one.
sim:
  batch: 64
# Q16.16 fixed point (see README.md). x/y are pixels, vx/vy pixels-per-frame.
name: MbBody
kind: component
fields:
  x:  { type: i32 }
  y:  { type: i32 }
  vx: { type: i32 }
  vy: { type: i32 }
# The fighter's decision state.
#
# bot_id indexes the bot table loaded from data/bots.yaml. policy_kind /
# policy_id are the STRATEGY the bot table last rolled: an MbPolicyKind index
# (default/spam/random/passive/perfect) plus, for spam, the move key it spams.
#
# hold counts down the chosen strategy's own decision cadence; bot_hold counts
# down the bot table's re-roll cadence (bots.hold_frames in app.yaml);
# was_busy is the busy-state edge that also forces a re-roll. plan_action /
# plan_left carry the random strategy's held pick. held_input is the latched
# MbInput bitfield (presses dropped, directions kept) replayed on non-decision
# frames.
#
# The exact order in which these consume RNG draws is the DRAW CONTRACT in
# ../CLAUDE.md - it is part of the determinism guarantee, not an
# implementation detail.
name: MbBrain
kind: component
fields:
  policy_kind: { type: i32 }
  policy_id:   { type: i32 }
  hold:        { type: i32 }
  plan_action: { type: i32 }
  plan_left:   { type: i32 }
  held_input:  { type: u32 }
  bot_id:      { type: i32 }
  bot_hold:    { type: i32 }
  was_busy:    { type: bool }
# Frame counters are whole frames. shake is Q16.16 (it decays by 0.5/frame).
# active is derived each tick by match_phase: true when this slot should be
# stepped by the fight-phase systems (phase == fight and not hit-frozen).
name: MbClock
kind: component
fields:
  frame:   { type: i32 }
  timer:   { type: i32 }
  round:   { type: i32 }
  phase:   { type: i32 }
  phase_t: { type: i32 }
  freeze:  { type: i32 }
  slowmo:  { type: i32 }
  shake:   { type: i32 }
  active:  { type: bool }
# Whole frames, one slot per move key. MB_MAX_MOVES must match this count
# (root CLAUDE.md decision 17: fixed bounds must match the data).
name: MbCooldowns
kind: component
fields:
  cd: { type: i32, count: 8 }
# THE CROSS (the Aistrop cheat). cheater/victim are side indices (0 or 1),
# -1 while no cross is running.
name: MbCross
kind: component
fields:
  t:          { type: i32 }
  window:     { type: i32 }
  cheater:    { type: i32 }
  victim:     { type: i32 }
  used:       { type: bool }
  puss_cross: { type: bool }
name: MbFighter
kind: marker
# Finisher window state. window_t and rearm are whole frames.
name: MbFinisher
kind: component
fields:
  window_t: { type: i32 }
  rearm:    { type: i32 }
  armed:    { type: bool }
  done:     { type: bool }
# All counters are whole frames (integers, not fixed point). state is an
# MbState index; facing is -1 or +1.
#
# attack_deferred is the one-frame handshake between the step_fighter and
# step_attack systems: set by step_fighter for a fighter that was ALREADY
# attacking (or holding a grab) when step_fighter ran, so step_attack owns
# that fighter's whole per-frame tail. A fighter that starts a move inside
# step_fighter is finished there, so a move's first frame is never stepped
# twice.
name: MbFsm
kind: component
fields:
  state:         { type: i32 }
  t:             { type: i32 }
  stun_t:        { type: i32 }
  dazed_t:       { type: i32 }
  invuln_frames: { type: i32 }
  hit_index:     { type: i32 }
  facing:        { type: i32 }
  block_stun:    { type: bool }
  prev_jump:     { type: bool }
  wall_jumped:   { type: bool }
  ripple:        { type: bool }
  attack_deferred: { type: bool }
# Grab hold state. foe is the held fighter entity; side_foe mirrors it as a
# side index (0/1) for the pure step functions, which never chase pointers.
name: MbGrab
kind: component
fields:
  t:        { type: i32 }
  move_id:  { type: i32 }
  side_foe: { type: i32 }
  foe:      { type: entity }
# Fixed ring of hazard slots (rats waves, coin zones, thrown objects).
# MB_HAZARD_CAP must match count: 8 (root CLAUDE.md decision 17 - an
# undersized bound silently drops state).
# dps/x/w/y/vy are Q16.16; count/kind/t/dur/owner are integers.
name: MbHazards
kind: component
fields:
  count: { type: i32 }
  kind:  { type: i32, count: 8 }
  t:     { type: i32, count: 8 }
  dur:   { type: i32, count: 8 }
  owner: { type: i32, count: 8 }
  dps:   { type: i32, count: 8 }
  x:     { type: i32, count: 8 }
  w:     { type: i32, count: 8 }
  y:     { type: i32, count: 8 }
  vy:    { type: i32, count: 8 }
# Bitfield: MB_IN_LEFT/RIGHT/JUMP/LIGHT/HEAVY/SPECIAL/BLOCK/STAKES_DOWN.
name: MbInput
kind: component
fields:
  bits: { type: u32 }
name: MbMatch
kind: marker
# move_id indexes the shared move table; move_key is the character-local slot
# (light / heavy / special / stakes_down). leap_vx is Q16.16.
name: MbMoveSlot
kind: component
fields:
  move_id:  { type: i32 }
  move_key: { type: i32 }
  leap_vx:  { type: i32 }
  proj_t:   { type: i32 }
# winner: 0 = side 0, 1 = side 1, -1 = no winner (drawn match, or unfinished).
name: MbOutcome
kind: component
fields:
  winner:     { type: i32 }
  frames:     { type: i32 }
  rounds:     { type: i32 }
  tie_rounds: { type: i32 }
  timeouts:   { type: i32 }
  finishers:  { type: i32 }
  capped:     { type: bool }
  drawn:      { type: bool }
# Canonical identity of the match occupying this slot. result_index is the
# position in the canonical (pairing, seed) result table - results are always
# aggregated in that order, never in completion order.
name: MbPair
kind: component
fields:
  entrant0:     { type: i32 }
  entrant1:     { type: i32 }
  char0:        { type: i32 }
  char1:        { type: i32 }
  seed_index:   { type: i32 }
  result_index: { type: i32 }
# Identity. slot is the batch slot index; side is 0 (left) or 1 (right). The
# pure step functions address a fighter as slot * 2 + side.
name: MbRefs
kind: component
fields:
  char_id:    { type: i32 }
  entrant_id: { type: i32 }
  slot:       { type: i32 }
  side:       { type: i32 }
  rounds_won: { type: i32 }
# Post-exchange shuffle (Brock) and the stakes-down walk-out (Aistrop).
name: MbRetreat
kind: component
fields:
  dir: { type: i32 }
  t:   { type: i32 }
# mulberry32 state, seeded from FNV-1a over "id0|id1|seed" exactly as
# test/tournament.js does in the JS reference. Draws are raw u32 words;
# probability thresholds are pre-scaled to 2^32, so no float ever appears.
name: MbRng
kind: component
fields:
  state: { type: u32 }
  seed:  { type: u32 }
# A request to admit one match to the hosted queue - the injection seam of the
# repo's ECS interaction pattern (root CLAUDE.md): tools/inject.py writes this
# component over REST, the WASM client will create the identical entity in
# process, and match_harvest consumes it on the next turn.
#
#   python3 tools/inject.py --template MbMatchRequest --set \
#     'MbSpawnRequest: {char0: "billy", bot0: "house", char1: "puss", bot1: "chaos", seed_index: 3}'
#
# WHY FOUR FIELDS AND NOT TWO ENTRANT IDS. The canonical entrant identity is
# the string "<char_id>:<bot_id>" (../CLAUDE.md, "Entrant identity"), and that
# is still what mb_engine.c composes and resolves - nothing about identity or
# seeding changes here. The two halves are carried separately because a
# component value has to survive tools/inject.py's `--set` mini-parser, which
# cannot express a value containing a colon. Splitting keeps the whole
# injection path usable from the CLI, which is the point of the pattern.
#
# NOT part of the batch schema in systems/native/mb_schema.c: no step function
# ever sees it, it is host-side scheduling only. Field ORDER and types are
# mirrored by MbSpawnRequestC in systems/native/mb_engine.c and the sizes are
# checked against each other at app start.
name: MbSpawnRequest
kind: component
fields:
  char0:      { type: string }
  bot0:       { type: string }
  char1:      { type: string }
  bot1:       { type: string }
  seed_index: { type: i32 }
# hp, damage_mult and chip_residual are Q16.16. chip_residual accumulates the
# numerator of per-frame divisions (grab damage, hazard chip, cross drain) so
# the total after N frames is exact - see README.md, "Accumulating divisions".
name: MbVitals
kind: component
fields:
  hp:            { type: i32 }
  damage_mult:   { type: i32 }
  chip_residual: { type: i32 }
  took_damage:   { type: bool }
  low_announced: { type: bool }
# target (a ring x position) and speed are Q16.16.
name: MbYank
kind: component
fields:
  target: { type: i32 }
  speed:  { type: i32 }
  active: { type: bool }
# monkey-baiting component schema and the integer-state contract

Every component in this directory is part of the **integer-state contract**:
no gameplay state is a float, anywhere, ever. This is not a style preference.
Bit-identical results are required across native x64, WASM, and (a definite
roadmap target) CUDA, and IEEE-754 arithmetic cannot deliver that across
those three toolchains. See
`web/monkey-baiting/docs/specs/tournament-simulator-architecture.md`
section 4.5, which inventories the 55 float-valued quantities in the JS
reference (`web/monkey-baiting/logic.js`) that this schema replaces.

## Fixed-point format

**Q16.16 in `int32_t`.** One unit = `1 << 16` = 65536.

| operation | expression |
|---|---|
| from integer | `v << 16` |
| multiply | `(int32_t)(((int64_t)a * (int64_t)b) >> 16)` |
| divide | `(int32_t)(((int64_t)a << 16) / b)` |
| to integer, truncating | `v >> 16` (arithmetic shift, floors toward -inf) |
| to integer, rounding | `(v + 32768) >> 16` |

Helpers live in `../systems/native/mb_fixed.h` (`MB_FP_ONE`, `mb_mul`,
`mb_div`, `mb_from_int`, `mb_to_int`, `mb_round_to_int`). Use them; do not
open-code the shifts.

Range: the ring is 348 px wide and velocities are single-digit px/frame, so
16 integer bits (+/- 32768) are ample and 16 fractional bits give 1/65536 px,
finer than any authored constant.

## Which fields are fixed point

Fixed point (Q16.16):

- `MbBody.x/y/vx/vy`
- `MbVitals.hp`, `MbVitals.damage_mult`, `MbVitals.chip_residual`
- `MbMoveSlot.leap_vx`
- `MbYank.target`, `MbYank.speed`
- `MbClock.shake`
- `MbHazards.dps/x/w/y/vy`

Plain integers (whole frames, indices, counts, side/slot ids, bitfields):
everything else. Frame counters are never fixed point - a frame is a frame.

## Precomputed at load, never computed at runtime

- **`cos(launch_angle)` and `sin(launch_angle)`** become Q16.16 constants in
  the shared move table when character data is loaded. There is no runtime
  trigonometry. `launch_angle` is authored per-move data, so this is free,
  and it removes the single largest cross-platform divergence risk (libm vs
  V8 vs a CUDA intrinsic all differ in the last ulp).
- **Health-fraction thresholds** (`low_health_announce * max_health`,
  finisher `health_threshold * max_health`, the cross trigger/min/cross
  health, `armor_if_health_above`, `ai.stakes_down_health`) become absolute
  Q16.16 hp values per character at load.
- **Probability thresholds** (`block_chance`, `retreat_chance`,
  `jump_chance`, the perfect-policy mixed strategy) become `u32` values
  pre-scaled to 2^32 and compared against a raw RNG word. The perfect
  policy's both-free distribution becomes a `u32` cumulative distribution
  summing to exactly 2^32, with the final bucket absorbing rounding.

All three landed in stage 2: the sin/cos table lives in
`../systems/native/mb_rules.c` (`mb_sin_q16`, whole degrees 0..90, rounded to
nearest - a table rather than libm because the three target libms disagree in
the last ulp), the health thresholds are resolved into `MbChar` fields
suffixed `_abs`, and the probabilities into `*_q32` fields.

## Accumulating divisions

Three quantities are divided every frame and applied to hp: per-frame grab
damage (`damage / grab_hold_frames`), hazard chip (`dps / logic_hz`), and
THE CROSS drain (`drain_per_second / logic_hz`). Rounding error there
compounds over thousands of frames and can flip a KO.

Treatment: accumulate the **numerator** in `MbVitals.chip_residual` (Q16.16)
and subtract whole units, so the total after N frames is exact rather than N
independently-rounded quotients.

Implemented as `mb_chip_apply` in `../systems/native/mb_step.c`, exported in
`mb_step.h` so it can be unit-tested directly. One residual per fighter is
shared by all three sources: they are additive damage against the same hp,
so a single carry is correct, and the invariant
`taken * denom + residual == numerator * frames` is asserted by
`tests/test_mb_sim.c`.

## Equality comparisons

The timeout comparison in the JS reference is
`player.hp / max === opp.hp / max` - an exact float equality that decides
drawn rounds. In C it is an integer cross-multiply:
`hp0 * max1 == hp1 * max0` on `int64_t`. Never divide to compare.

## Layout notes

All numeric fields are 4 bytes (`i32`/`u32`) and flags are `bool`. The
architecture doc budgets ~512 B per match assuming packed `i16`/`u8`; this
schema is roughly double that because the engine's YAML component format has
no narrow integer types and a single width keeps the C structs, the YAML
mirror, and the future CUDA columns trivially in agreement. Memory is not a
constraint at any foreseeable volume (the RAM ceiling is ~10^8 concurrent
matches); packing is a measured optimization for later, not a stage-1 one.

## Two declarations, one schema

These YAML files are the on-disk source of truth (root `CLAUDE.md` decision
2). The simulator also needs the same structs in C
(`../systems/native/mb_types.h`), because the hot path cannot go through
runtime reflection. `mb_world_bind` resolves each component by name and
**asserts the registered type size equals `sizeof` the C struct**, and
`tests/test_mb_sim.c` parses every file in this directory and compares field
names, types and array counts against the C descriptor table. Drift between
the two fails the tests rather than silently corrupting state.
# monkey-baiting sandbox data

This directory is the **owning copy** of the game's authored data:

- `characters/*.yaml` - character stats, mechanics, `ai:` block and move frame
  data. Verbatim in content from the JS reference
  (`web/monkey-baiting/data/characters/`); this copy is what the C simulator
  loads and what balance changes are made against.
- `strategies.yaml` - the primitive decision methods and their params, plus
  each strategy's decision cadence.
- `bots.yaml` - the bot weight/proximity tables over those strategies.

Global tuning and game structure (ring, rounds, combat constants, finisher,
AI shape constants, roster order) do not live here: they are in `../app.yaml`,
which is the sandbox config file the engine and the simulator both read.

## Scaling

Fractional values are authored as decimal strings and converted **once, at
load**, to Q16.16 signed fixed point (one unit = 65536) by exact integer
arithmetic in `../systems/native/mb_rules.c` (`mb_parse_fp` - no `strtod`, no
float anywhere in the pipeline). The decimal string is the authoring format
precisely because it stays readable and diffable; the integer contract is
enforced at the boundary, not in the file. Whole-frame counters, pixel
bounds, indices and counts stay plain integers.

Three further conversions happen at load, so nothing is computed on the hot
path (see `../components/README.md`):

- `launch_angle` (whole degrees, 0..90) becomes a Q16.16 sin/cos pair from a
  fixed table. There is no runtime trigonometry on any target.
- Health fractions (`mechanics.cross_health`, `mechanics.the_cross.
  trigger_health`, `armor_if_health_above`, `ai.stakes_down_health`) become
  absolute Q16.16 hp against that character's `max_health`.
- Probabilities (`ai.block_chance`, `retreat_chance`, `jump_chance`,
  `stance_chance`, the strategy `*_chance` params, and the derived
  `aggression`-scaled rolls) become u32 thresholds pre-scaled to 2^32 and are
  compared against a raw mulberry32 word.

Bot weights in `bots.yaml` must be **positive integers**: the roll is an
integer draw over the eligible weight sum, walked against prefix sums.

## Adding data

Every key the loader reads is required at its own level and fails the load by
name when absent (root `CLAUDE.md` decision 12). A new gameplay value means:
add it to the YAML, add its read to `mb_rules.c`, add its field to
`MbRules`/`MbChar`/`MbMove`. Never type a number into `mb_step.c`.

`web/monkey-baiting/data/` remains the JS reference's own data. It is
read-only reference material and must not be edited to suit the port; the
port does not track it and does not diff against it.
# MONKEY BAITING -- balance grammar (analysis layer, NOT shipped)
#
# The machine-readable form of the balance playbook (docs/balance.md section 6).
# This file is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for balance strategies; section 6
# keeps the concept and a one-line index only.
#
# CANONICAL LOCATION (2026-07-30, architecture S5 data single-sourcing): this
# file was `web/monkey-baiting/test/balance-grammar.yaml`. It now lives with
# the rest of the authored monkey-baiting data in
# `sandboxes/monkey-baiting/data/`, which is THE authoring location for the
# port. It is read by the Python loop (`tools/mb_loop/grammar.py`) only; the C
# simulator never loads it and nothing in it is a runtime number.
#
# GRAMMAR SHAPE
#   version       grammar version (balance v0, v1, ...). Re-runs under a newer
#                 grammar re-score earlier rounds, so the number is part of any
#                 recorded result.
#   situations[]  id, summary, detect[]        -- what the tools can SEE
#   strategies[]  id, situation, lever, rationale, evidence[], patch (optional)
#                                              -- what to DO
#   compounds[]   id, case, members[]          -- recurring merges (MERGE RULE)
#   scoring       per-situation weights for the scalar balance score
#
# detect[] entries are {source, pattern}. The source list is an OPEN ENUM:
# new sources are expected (moveset telemetry -- active-move and
# passive-movement distribution percentages; match-rules variants Bo1/Bo3/Bo5).
# A source test/grammar.js does not know yet is legal; it validates the pattern
# is a map, reports "no detector" and contributes no signals. Known sources and
# their validated pattern fields:
#   tournament-issues   category: <ISSUES prefix>   match: <substring, optional>
#   spam-parity         band: [lo, hi]  direction: above|below|outside
#                       requires: framedata (optional)  top_dominance: N (optional)
#   standings-bands     metric: bot-label-aggregate|perfect-matchup|default-matchup
#                       band: [lo, hi]  label_prefix: <string, optional>
#   framedata-envelope  min_violations: N   field: any|<envelope field>
#   solver-matrix       manual: true  rule: <text>   (no structured feed yet)
#   solver-convergence  manual: true  rule: <text>   (no structured feed yet)
#
# MERGE RULE (see docs/balance.md section 6): strategies are per-situation and
# situations recur. When the same case keeps taking the same combination of
# strategies, add a COMPOUND here listing the member ids in application order
# and cite the compound id thereafter. The compound documents the recurring
# combination; it never replaces its members. Compound shape:
#   compounds:
#     - id: armored-outlier-pass
#       case: >
#         a solver-flagged armor trade that also shows as degenerate spam
#       members: [triage-resolution, single-button-value]
#
# A detected situation with no strategy referencing it is a PLAYBOOK GAP:
# per the REQUIRED rule, write the strategy before making the change.
#
# PATCH TEMPLATES (strategy.patch, optional -- null for analysis-only
# strategies): the machine-appliable shape of the change a strategy emits, for
# the eventual automated loop (propose patch -> re-run tournament -> score ->
# accept/reject). Fields:
#   layer      same enum as lever.layer
#   file       the data/ YAML the patch writes (pattern, <char> is a slot)
#   path       parameter path pattern inside that file
#   direction  raise | lower | toward-envelope | toward-parity | clamp-to-envelope
#   format     overrides-partial-yaml (the --overrides directory pattern)
# A patch is a TEMPLATE, not a change: the slots are filled from the detected
# signal (which move, which flagged parameter).
#
# CHANGELOG (versioned grammar -- a scoring change or new situation is a new
# version; see docs/balance.md section 6):
#   v3 (2026-07-29): add situation `design-constraint-adoption` and strategy
#     `design-constraint` -- a recordable class for human, out-of-band design
#     rulings (pins/envelopes/universal mechanics adopted from
#     docs/specs/starting-constraints.md), so such changes have a ledger
#     citation. patch: null and scoring weight 0, same treatment as
#     `model-fix`/`must-not-lose-failure`: never emitted by the loop, never
#     scored. First instance: uniform-health (ruling 8, aistrop max_health
#     110 -> 100).

version: 3

situations:

  - id: degenerate-spam-dominance
    summary: >
      a spam-X bot beats its own character's default AI, or sits far above the
      spam-parity band
    detect:
      - source: tournament-issues
        pattern: {category: DEGENERATE}
      - source: spam-parity
        pattern: {band: [45, 55], direction: above}

  - id: class-wide-band-violation
    summary: >
      a whole bot class (pooled by bot label, e.g. every character's
      spam-light) sits outside the acceptance band
    detect:
      - source: standings-bands
        pattern: {metric: bot-label-aggregate, label_prefix: spam-, band: [45, 55]}
      - source: framedata-envelope
        pattern: {min_violations: 1, field: any}

  - id: static-realized-disagreement
    summary: >
      static per-exchange dominance ranks a move at the top but its spam bot
      finishes mid-table or below (or the reverse)
    detect:
      - source: spam-parity
        pattern: {band: [45, 55], direction: below, requires: framedata, top_dominance: 3}

  - id: solver-result-suspicious
    summary: >
      tier-0 says perfect play loses a matchup it should not, or an
      equilibrium value looks implausible
    detect:
      - source: solver-matrix
        pattern:
          manual: true
          rule: >
            perfect loses to its own character's spam or default, or a value
            moves materially between quantization levels

  - id: solver-nonconvergence
    summary: >
      value-iteration delta plateaus at the same value regardless of the
      iteration budget
    detect:
      - source: solver-convergence
        pattern:
          manual: true
          rule: >
            identical final delta at 400 and 4000 iters for the same matchup

  - id: standoff-vs-lopsided
    summary: >
      the solver calls a matchup near zero but the real engine resolves it
      one-sidedly (the watch decides)
    detect:
      - source: standings-bands
        pattern: {metric: perfect-matchup, band: [25, 75]}
      - source: solver-matrix
        pattern:
          manual: true
          rule: >
            equilibrium value within +/-1 damage at round start

  - id: character-band-violation
    summary: >
      a character's same-type win rate (default-vs-default or
      perfect-vs-perfect) is outside the acceptance band
    detect:
      - source: tournament-issues
        pattern: {category: CHARACTER}

  - id: matchup-blowout
    summary: >
      a default-vs-default matchup falls outside the 25-75 band
    detect:
      - source: tournament-issues
        pattern: {category: MATCHUP}

  - id: spam-pressure-foreign-default
    summary: >
      a spam bot beats another character's tuned default AI by 75% or more
    detect:
      - source: tournament-issues
        pattern: {category: SPAM PRESSURE}

  - id: stall-timeouts
    summary: >
      an entrant ends more than 20% of its rounds on the watch
    detect:
      - source: tournament-issues
        pattern: {category: TIMEOUTS}

  - id: frame-cap-stalemate
    summary: >
      a match hits the frame cap without resolving
    detect:
      - source: tournament-issues
        pattern: {category: FRAME CAP}

  - id: must-not-lose-failure
    summary: >
      a solved perfect bot loses any match to its own character's spam or
      default bots at full resolution (the acceptance rule the solver model
      must satisfy; survives-resolution failures are model defects)
    detect:
      - source: own-bot-matchups   # detector pending in grammar.js: read the
        pattern:                   # win-rate matrix rows <char>/perfect vs
          opponent_types: [spam, default]   # <char>/spam-*, <char>/default
          threshold: 100           # anything below 100% of decided = failure
      - source: solver-matrix
        pattern:
          manual: true
          rule: >
            perfect vs own spam/default loses at full resolution after
            triage-resolution ruled out quantization

  - id: design-constraint-adoption
    summary: >
      a human, out-of-band design ruling (pins, envelopes, universal
      mechanics) is adopted as cast-wide policy -- never detected from
      tournament/solver telemetry; this situation exists only so the
      resulting data change has a playbook home and a ledger citation
    detect:
      # detector pending in grammar.js, same open-enum treatment as
      # must-not-lose-failure's own-bot-matchups: adoption is a project-
      # owner decision recorded in a spec doc, never inferred from a report.
      - source: design-ruling
        pattern:
          manual: true
          rule: >
            adoption is recorded in a decision doc (e.g.
            docs/specs/starting-constraints.md), never inferred from a
            tournament or solver report

strategies:

  - id: triage-resolution
    situation: solver-result-suspicious
    lever:
      layer: model-mechanics
      change: >
        re-solve at finer quantization first; only if the anomaly survives,
        fix the modeled mechanics on the node side
    rationale: >
      Never treat a solver artifact as a balance finding. A coarse grid can
      both invent an edge and hide one, so resolution has to be ruled out
      before the result is allowed to name a tuning target. What survives
      full resolution is a MODEL gap, not a character problem.
    patch: null   # analysis-only: emits a re-solve, never a data change
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: inner-solve-first
    situation: solver-nonconvergence
    lever:
      layer: solver-params
      change: >
        raise --fp-rounds (the per-state fictitious-play matrix solve), not
        --iters; if the plateau survives a large fp increase, accept
        best-effort and note which states cycle
    rationale: >
      A delta that ignores the iteration budget is not an under-iterated
      outer loop, it is an under-solved inner matrix game. More sweeps of a
      wrong per-state backup buy nothing. A plateau that survives a large fp
      increase is a genuine value cycle in the game model.
    patch: null   # analysis-only: changes solver flags, not game data
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: single-button-value
    situation: degenerate-spam-dominance
    lever:
      layer: move-param
      change: >
        tune the move's flagged envelope outlier (the parameter static
        analysis names), not the character's overall numbers
    rationale: >
      Degenerate dominance means one button packs too much value at once
      (armor plus damage, safety plus stagger). Static frame analysis names
      the offending parameter; the tournament confirms whether the value is
      actually realized. Character-wide nudges would move four healthy moves
      to fix one.
    patch:
      layer: move-param
      file: data/characters/<char>.yaml
      path: moves.<move>.<flagged_param>
      direction: toward-envelope
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: realized-value
    situation: static-realized-disagreement
    lever:
      layer: move-param
      change: >
        tune on realized (tournament) value; use static dominance only to
        pick which parameter to touch
    rationale: >
      Static dominance is per-exchange threat with cooldowns ignored; the
      tournament is value realized over a full match including cooldowns and
      whiffs. When they disagree, the match-long number is the one the player
      experiences.
    patch:
      layer: move-param
      file: data/characters/<char>.yaml
      path: moves.<move>.<dominance_driver_param>
      direction: toward-parity
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: timer-standoff
    situation: standoff-vs-lopsided
    lever:
      layer: objective
      change: >
        do not tune the characters; treat it as an objective/model question
        (timeout modeling) or accept it as timer identity
    rationale: >
      The damage-differential objective deliberately omits the round timer,
      so a near-zero value means "neither side can force profit", not "even
      and active". The watch then decides the real match. Tuning characters
      to close that gap would be tuning to an artifact of the objective.
    patch: null   # deliberately no-tune: the lever is the objective, not data
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: class-envelope
    situation: class-wide-band-violation
    lever:
      layer: class-envelope
      change: >
        change the class box in data/balance.yaml (e.g. an on-block safety
        floor for lights) rather than making one nudge per character
    rationale: >
      A symptom shared by a whole class is a class problem. Per-character
      tuning is for outliers inside a healthy class; applied to a class-wide
      symptom it produces five correlated changes that all have to be
      re-derived on the next pass. Parity of outcome, not homogeneity of
      numbers: the envelope keeps the shape variety.
    patch:
      layer: class-envelope
      file: data/balance.yaml
      path: classes.<class>.<param>
      direction: toward-parity
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: power-budget
    situation: character-band-violation
    lever:
      layer: move-param
      change: >
        sample the character's highest-dominance moves (static pass names
        them) toward the band -- nerf when above, buff when below -- spread
        across the outlier moves rather than one lever
    rationale: >
      A character-level symptom is a power-budget problem, not one move's.
      The static dominance ranking says where the budget is concentrated;
      moving the top outliers a little each preserves the moveset's shape
      while shifting the total. Exploration samples within this restriction
      rather than prescribing amounts.
    patch:
      layer: move-param
      file: data/characters/<char>.yaml
      path: moves.<top_dominance_move>.<param>
      direction: toward-parity
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: matchup-counterplay
    situation: matchup-blowout
    lever:
      layer: move-param
      change: >
        only when NEITHER character violates its own band: sample the
        pairing-specific interaction parameters (reach vs the opponent's
        poke range, blockstun vs their punish window) named by that
        pairing's fight records
    rationale: >
      Fix character terms before pairwise terms: a blowout between two
      band-violating characters is usually their budgets, not the pairing
      (defer to power-budget; natural compound if the deferral recurs). A
      blowout between two otherwise-healthy characters is a genuine
      interaction problem, and the fight records name the interaction.
    patch:
      layer: move-param
      file: data/characters/<char>.yaml
      path: moves.<move>.<interaction_param>
      direction: toward-parity
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: defense-gap
    situation: spam-pressure-foreign-default
    lever:
      layer: ai-param
      change: >
        route first: if the attacking move is also spam-parity-high, it is
        the move (single-button-value). Otherwise sample the VICTIM'S
        default-AI parameters (blocking rates, ranges in data/strategies.yaml
        / bots.yaml) -- an AI hole, not a stats hole
    rationale: >
      A tuned default AI that loses 75%+ to one-button pressure usually
      cannot answer the pattern (never blocks it, wrong range), and fixing
      the AI does not touch any character's power budget -- the cheapest
      possible fix with zero balance side effects. Aistrop's default was
      the poster case.
    patch:
      layer: ai-param
      file: data/strategies.yaml
      path: <victim_default_strategy>.<ai_param>
      direction: toward-parity
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: engagement
    situation: stall-timeouts
    lever:
      layer: move-param
      change: >
        sample the stalling character's engagement costs toward cheaper
        engagement (whiff recovery, approach displacement, chip on block)
        within envelopes; the match-rules timer dimension is a separate
        lever once match rules are config
    rationale: >
      Distinct from timer-standoff by data source: timer-standoff is
      equilibrium camping under the solver's timerless objective (no-tune);
      stall-timeouts is EMPIRICAL bot behavior in real timed matches, and
      is tunable. A character who stalls because committing is bad value
      needs commitment made cheaper, not a lecture.
    patch:
      layer: move-param
      file: data/characters/<char>.yaml
      path: moves.<move>.<engagement_param>
      direction: toward-parity
      format: overrides-partial-yaml
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: stalemate-break
    situation: frame-cap-stalemate
    lever:
      layer: match-rules
      change: >
        rules-layer first (sudden death / damage decay in the match rules),
        never character data; if it ever fires alongside stall-timeouts,
        compound with engagement
    rationale: >
      A match that hits the frame cap without resolving is a rules hole,
      not a tuning target -- two healthy characters can still refuse to
      interact. Zero instances detected to date; this entry exists so the
      situation is covered when match rules become config (the v8 tie
      rules -- max_rounds_per_match cap, drawn matches -- are the first
      instance of this layer in the shipped data).
    patch: null   # rules-layer: emits a match-rules config change, not data
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: model-fix
    situation: must-not-lose-failure
    lever:
      layer: model-mechanics
      change: >
        diagnose the losing exchange (fight records) to find which engine
        mechanic the solver model mispredicts; fix the node-side model
        (test/solver.js + test/solver-export.js, kept consistent), re-export,
        re-solve, re-run the acceptance tournament
    rationale: >
      Perfect play is only as perfect as the model it was solved in. A
      must-not-lose failure that survives full resolution is a defect in the
      measuring instrument, not in the character data -- tuning around it
      would bake the model's error into the game. Fix the model, then let
      every perfect-derived signal be re-earned by a fresh solve. First
      instance: the armor-trade gap (committed-move-rearm assumption vs
      armor windows; jacco spam-light/heavy, puss armored heavy, aistrop
      cane, 2026-07-29). If this case recurs behind triage-resolution, merge
      them as a compound per the MERGE RULE.
    patch: null   # emits a CODE change to the solver model, never a data patch
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

  - id: design-constraint
    situation: design-constraint-adoption
    lever:
      layer: no-tune
      change: >
        record the human ruling and the resulting hand-made data change in
        the tuning ledger, citing this strategy; the change itself is made
        by hand against the decision doc, never proposed or emitted by the
        automated loop
    rationale: >
      A design constraint (pins, envelopes, universal mechanics) is decided
      by the project owner, not detected from a tournament or solver report,
      and it is not a tuning target for the explorer to rediscover or
      revert -- same non-tunable status as a pin. It still needs a home in
      the ledger so every data change (not just automated ones) is
      traceable to a citation. First instance: uniform-health (ruling 8,
      docs/specs/starting-constraints.md, 2026-07-29) -- aistrop's
      max_health 110 -> 100, adopted cast-wide.
    patch: null   # never emitted: no automated lever, this is a human ruling
    evidence:
      - 2026-07-29

# No compounds yet. Add one when the same case repeatedly takes the same
# ordered combination of the strategies above; see the MERGE RULE header.
compounds: []

# ---- scoring ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The scalar balance score for a report: sum over situations of
# weight x signal-count. LOWER IS BETTER (zero = every band holds). Computed
# from the SAME detected situations as the table above, so the optimization
# loop scores exactly what the playbook reacts to. Situations with no weight
# entry use default_weight. Manual and undetectable-this-run situations
# contribute nothing (they are reported separately, never silently scored).
scoring:
  default_weight: 1
  weights:
    degenerate-spam-dominance: 3
    character-band-violation: 3
    class-wide-band-violation: 2
    matchup-blowout: 2
    spam-pressure-foreign-default: 1
    stall-timeouts: 1
    frame-cap-stalemate: 5
    standoff-vs-lopsided: 1
    static-realized-disagreement: 1
    # Oracle-integrity signal, deliberately weight 0: a model defect must
    # GATE perfect-derived signals (fix the model first), never be a number
    # the tuning loop can "improve" by moving character data.
    must-not-lose-failure: 0
    # Never auto-detected (source has no structured feed by design) and
    # never scored: a design-constraint adoption is a human ruling, not a
    # symptom the loop measures or a target it may revert.
    design-constraint-adoption: 0
# MONKEY BAITING -- balance pins (analysis-layer constraint, NOT shipped)
#
# CANONICAL LOCATION (2026-07-30, architecture S5 data single-sourcing): this
# file was `web/monkey-baiting/test/balance-pins.yaml`. It now lives with the
# rest of the authored monkey-baiting data in `sandboxes/monkey-baiting/data/`.
# It is read only by the Python loop's legal-patch verifier
# (`tools/mb_loop/patch.py`). No runtime code reads pins.
#
# A pin locks a specific parameter (or a whole file, path: "*") out of the
# explorer's sample space: any candidate patch touching a pinned location is
# INVALID regardless of score. Pins are human-authored (playtesting/design
# judgement), never written by the loop itself. See docs/specs/loop-emitter.md
# "Pins file" and docs/balance-loop.md section 1.
#
# Match rule: a pin blocks an edit when `file` is equal AND (`path` is "*" OR
# the edit path equals the pin path OR the edit path is a dotted descendant of
# it). All four fields (id, reason, file, path) are required; unknown fields
# fail validation (test/patch.js loadPins). NOTE: this flat {id, reason, file,
# path} shape -- not the "sets" grouping (id/always/reason/game/characters/
# moves/params) sketched in docs/specs/starting-constraints.md section 4 -- is
# the one test/patch.js actually loads and enforces (see loop-emitter.md
# "Pins file" for the authoritative example). The sets below are populated as
# individual flat pin entries; the set groupings (P0/P1/P2/P3) are recorded
# only as comment headers so the provenance stays traceable to
# docs/specs/starting-constraints.md section 5.
#
# Populated per docs/specs/starting-constraints.md (status DECIDED 2026-07-29),
# rulings 1-8. Active sets below: P0 `universal-defense` (always on), P1
# `identity-guards` (always on), P3 `ai-frozen` (adopted for first runs, must
# stay droppable -- see note below). NOT included as active pins:
#
# - P2 `reference-jacco` (ruling 1: NEVER a default; cataloged optional
#   anchoring experiment only). Would be: file data/characters/jacco.yaml,
#   path "*". Left uncited here on purpose -- this flat schema has no
#   always:false/on-off toggle, every listed pin is unconditionally enforced,
#   so including it would make jacco reference-locked by default, which
#   ruling 1 explicitly forbids. To run the `reference-jacco` sibling lineage,
#   pass a separate --pins fixture (see loop-emitter.md CLI --pins FILE) that
#   layers this one entry on top of P0+P1, rather than editing this file.
# - P0 `uniform-health` (ruling 8, design constraint, ACTIVE below). Both
#   gates are satisfied: (a) aistrop max_health 110->100 with the
#   "2026-07-29 -- aistrop/max_health" ledger entry in docs/balance.md
#   section 5 (citing the `design-constraint` strategy, grammar v3), and
#   (b) the full-res 5-pair aistrop re-solve ran 2026-07-29 (converged,
#   delta <= 0.02 all pairs; policies swapped into policies-full-v3 and
#   accepted via a clean tournament load). max_health is pinned per
#   character below (uniform-health-* entries).
#
# P3 `ai-frozen` must remain tunable/droppable like any other pin set (ruling
# 7): drop the 5 "ai-frozen-*" entries below (or point a run at a --pins file
# that omits them) once frame data settles and the `defense-gap` strategy
# needs ai.* unpinned.

version: 1
pins:
